   "headingEn": "Why does English get away without this?",
   "en": "English used to do this too — Old English adjectives had endings. Over centuries English wore them off, so 'new' now sits still: new house, new car, new window. Russian kept its endings, so the adjective still points at its noun and says 'I go with that one.' That pointing is useful when word order gets loose."
