   "headingEn": "Why \"we with you\" for \"you and I\"?",
   "en": "«Мы с тобой» literally means \"we with you.\" Russian bundles both people into the \"we\" first, then names the companion. It feels warm and togetherness-first, where English lines the two of you up side by side. Same meaning, different camera angle.",
   "noteEn": "So \"you and I\" is heard as «мы с тобой» (my s taboy), not a word-for-word match."
