   "en": "Old Russian had several noun classes, each with its own plural genitive. Modern Russian kept the leftovers: hard masculines clung to -ов, soft nouns to -ей, and the -а/-о nouns lost their ending entirely. The last letter of the dictionary form is your fossil clue to which old class a word belonged to."
